[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1929","stub":{"id":"1929","section":"0.3 Pinnacles Power Plant to Claudville","altname":"Kibler Valley","river":"Dan","difficulty":"ItoIII","states":["USA-VRG"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"91","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"Main put-in during generation. Gates are locked at night and no cars should be parked here. Small parking spot just below gate.","id":"104980","location":{"latitude":"36.6663","longitude":"-80.448299"},"name":"Powerplant","distance":"0.02","photoID":"50120","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"playspot","description":"Referred to simply as Public Enemy. This one comes up fast after a braided riffle. Easiest route begins in mid-channel and running slightly right of mid-current, but the eddy line is strong at times.  A more adventurous line is the far left line \"slot\", along the bank through small boulders.  Depending on flow, a quick right turn is needed to avoid boulders as this line has been responsible for a few boat pins.","id":"2703","location":{"latitude":"36.66419982910156","longitude":"-80.44940185546875"},"name":"Public Enemy # 2","distance":"0.21","difficulty":"III","approximate":false,"photoID":"50121"},{"type":"playspot","description":"Not really a standalone rapid, but noteworthy as a fun feature and small playspot.  To catch it, run far river right after the boulder jumble below the first bridge.  There is a big eddy with good service running along a low cliff face.  Decent for enders in short boats.  A wave forms mid-channel during most runnable flows.","id":"103140","location":{"latitude":"36.66299819946289","longitude":"-80.4489974975586"},"name":"(Sammy's) Boof Rock","distance":"0.27","difficulty":"II","approximate":false,"photoID":"50124"},{"type":"put-in","description":"The is one everyone watches from the old basketball court.  Just below the boof rock, work towards the middle and shoot down the lower end of the rapid.  Good eddy on right side behind the boulder for rescue set-up or raft play.  To avoid flipping, simply angle left of the boulder and paddle hard.  Sliding into the rock on on accident pretty much guarantees a swim.  Folks wanting to avoid the bigger rapids can put-in here via a small trail.","id":"103139","location":{"latitude":"36.66270065307617","longitude":"-80.44920349121094"},"name":"Basketball Falls","distance":"0.28","photoID":"884149","difficulty":"III","approximate":false},{"type":"playspot","description":"Current is split by a large boulder on the upstream side of a small island.  Rapid at the bottom.  Left channel is deeper.  Rapid can be avoided by taking the right channel, though a little shallow.  A small surf ledge can be played in.","id":"104976","location":{"latitude":"36.66130065917969","longitude":"-80.44760131835938"},"name":"Island Rapid","distance":"0.47","difficulty":"II","approximate":false,"photoID":"0"},{"type":"playspot","description":"Below 2nd bridge. Popular playspot.","id":"105307","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Cabin Rapid","distance":"0.8","difficulty":"I","approximate":true,"photoID":"0"},{"type":"playspot","description":"Nice little wave to have fun in, just after the river opening under the powerline.  Named in \"honor\" of paddler Joanne Kizer who got a nice cut while playing in this spot back in '06.  The narrow right side riffled channel can be run during most generations.","id":"104977","location":{"latitude":"36.654300689697266","longitude":"-80.44730377197266"},"name":"Seven Stitches","distance":"1.09","difficulty":"II","approximate":false,"photoID":"0"},{"type":"playspot","description":"Popular playspot behind Barnards store.  Named after the numerous old cars that used to be visible, all cleaned up now.  Last decent rapid before the main takeout.","id":"104978","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Junkyard Hole","distance":"3.67","difficulty":"I","approximate":false,"photoID":"0"},{"type":"takeout","description":"Please pull of the shoulder and avoid parking at the church (especially on Sundays). Small trail leads into river-left calm eddy.","id":"104979","location":{"latitude":"36.627384","longitude":"-80.44916"},"name":"Takeout - Danube Church","distance":"3.94","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[{"id":"50124","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"52397","description":"Boof above Basketball Falls","caption":"Boof above Basketball Falls","poi_name":"Basketball Falls","subject":"Garrick","author":"Alyssa Taylor","poi_id":"103139","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/50124","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/50124"}},{"id":"50121","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"52397","description":"Rapid above the first bridge.  Tight slot river left.  Boof river center and a tongue out of the picture on river right.","caption":"Rapid above first Bridge","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Alyssa Taylor","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/50121","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/50121"}},{"id":"50122","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"52397","description":"Centerline on rapid above the first bridge","caption":"Boof on rapid above first bridge","poi_name":"","subject":"Rick","author":"Alyssa Taylor","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/50122","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/50122"}},{"id":"50126","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"52397","description":"Basketball Falls","caption":"Basketball falls","poi_name":"Basketball Falls","subject":"Keith","author":"Alyssa Taylor","poi_id":"103139","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/50126","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/50126"}},{"id":"50935","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"877238","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/50935","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/50935"}},{"id":"833420","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"833421","description":"","caption":"Heading down to basketball falls","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/833420","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/833420"}},{"id":"833466","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"833467","description":"James on Kibler","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/833466","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/833466"}},{"id":"50123","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"52397","description":"Tailrace - good place to practice ferries and surfing","caption":"Tailrace","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Alyssa Taylor","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/50123","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/50123"}},{"id":"50120","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"52397","description":"Tailrace - good place to practice ferries and surfing","caption":"Tailrace","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Alyssa Taylor","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/50120","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/50120"}},{"id":"50934","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"877237","description":"Appears to be good flow in this shot, washing out the boof. -BLP","caption":"Looking down Basketball.","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/50934","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/50934"}},{"id":"884149","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"886845","description":"'Blue' ferrying across tongue, 'yellow' and 'green' resting in the boulder eddy.  This is a low level for playing as the wave was hard to catch.","caption":"Basketball","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Brandon Levi","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/884149","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/884149"}},{"id":"50933","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"877236","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/50933","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/50933"}},{"id":"50127","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"52397","description":"Riding the tongue above Basketball Falls","caption":"Riding the tongue above Basketball Falls","poi_name":"Basketball Falls","subject":"Marc","author":"Alyssa Taylor","poi_id":"103139","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/50127","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/50127"}},{"id":"hmmrumcVEvEN4TAnPkGMm","ext":"jpg","post_id":"Ua0N5tjPRJ6TuDVxHKELT","description":"","caption":"Kibler Fest parking","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Philip Young","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2022-07-17T12:12:25.000Z","updated_at":"2022-07-17T12:12:25.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/hmmrumcVEvEN4TAnPkGMm","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/hmmrumcVEvEN4TAnPkGMm"}},{"id":"886307","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"889111","description":"Whitewater SUP in Kibler Valley\r\nThe local paddling community has taken to calling the boof rock at Basketball, Sammy's Boof, in memory of a well-loved and long-time Kibler Valley boater.","caption":"Ollie-boof at Basketball Falls","poi_name":"Basketball Falls","subject":"Dale Swanson","author":"Delane Heath","poi_id":"103139","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/886307","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/886307"}},{"id":"KCkYef9C4Pq875yUmqqdk","ext":"jpg","post_id":"Ua0N5tjPRJ6TuDVxHKELT","description":"","caption":"Kibler Fest finish","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Philip Young","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2022-07-17T12:12:25.000Z","updated_at":"2022-07-17T12:12:25.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/KCkYef9C4Pq875yUmqqdk","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/KCkYef9C4Pq875yUmqqdk"}}],"detail":{"id":"1929","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"The Kibler Valley is a popular summer tailrace run beginning at the Pinnacles Powerplant downstream of Townes Reservoir.  While the water is plenty cold during generation, it is not shockingly frigid like the Nantahala or nearby Smith River.  Boaters are encouraged to call the powerplant (276-215-5141) for release schedule as there is rarely enough 'freestone' water to paddle without a major precipitation event. (See flow and gauge info).  7500kW is considered a minimum by most local paddlers.\n\nThere are several nice whitewater features near the top, and the narrow boulder-laden channel provides a few strong eddy lines, hidden rocks, and tricky currents.  The river gradually gentles with each mile and significant rapids all but dissappear after 3 miles (4th bridge) under normal conditions.  The lower segments attract large numbers of both recreational paddlers and tubers, particularly during hot weekends when water is released.\n\nWhile the road follows the river very closely on the upper half of this run, much of the riverbank property is prominently posted against trespass.  Please respect private property rights and only park in non-posted pulloffs. Paddler's can expect a mixed reception as some landowners will wave and smile while others think that paddling should be banned entirely on this stretch. Though the Danube Church is a well-known meeting spot, locals are requesting boaters not to keep vehicles in the parking lot.  Simply pull out and park on the shoulder.  -BLP Aug. 2014\n\nAs of June 2016, the 'public' campsites are not available for use.\n\n_Eric O'Connell provides (2003-10-12):_\n\n_I ran Dan 'Creek', Saturday October 12 at 1:00. The Level was 152 CFS which is about 13cfs too low, however it still was a great class 2 with three rapids rating class 3. I took my girlfriend on this trip for her first river expedition and she had a blast. I recomend this trip for beginners and intermediates wanting to sharpen their skills. With a bit more water this would be a prime river run. I advise you to call before driving to see if the water level is sufficent to run the river. If so try it out and you will not be let down._\n\n_Put In: The Pinnacles Power Plant is very good at allowing boaters to park at their facility. They also know the river really well and can answer many questions about the trip._ _The first half mile in my opinion is the best, in fact we got out and ran that section twice. The road runs right along the river for the entire run and is very accessible._\n\n_100 yards from the put in is a good class 3 rapid that is easliy run from river right around two medium boulders that will flip you if given the opportunity. If you do get flipped there is a small pool below this rapid that you can roll before the next rapids._\n\n_Another 100 yards downstream there is a good class 3 rapid on river left that is run easily if you start river right and work river left. If is very fun and usually there are people on the bridge watching you blast the rapid._\n\n_From here you will take a 2 mile journey filled with fast, steady whitewater with two or three more good class 3 rapids. There were no hazards on my trip but still be cautious because this creek is very fast and there is little time to scout and very few places to relax. This is a very quiet creek and all the landowners are very polite and enjoy having our company, please be polite. (section removed, BLP 2016)_\n\nSee Also:\n\n_Virginia Whitewater_, Roger Corbett (2000 ed.), p. 505.\n\n_Exploring Virginia's Waterways_, Ed Gertler (2022 ed.), p. 266.","averageGradient":"35","maxGradient":"75","length":"11.2","correlations":[{"reachID":"1929","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"200","beginMediumRunnable":"300","beginHighRunnable":"1000","endHighRunnable":"2000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"DAN RIVER NEAR FRANCISCO, NC","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"02068500","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"91","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"1.49","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"36.515","longitude":"-80.3030556"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/02068500","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/02068500/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":"This gauge is around 20 miles below the run so there is a significant time lag as well as exaggerated flow rates due to additional 